1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:06,660
AgentLedger is a social DApp prototype for agent developers and operators.

2
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:13,100
It connects finding a collaborator, agreeing on responsibilities, and acknowledging shared work.

3
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:22,760
We will follow one example collaboration, then show separate evidence of real wallet interactions and optional payment settlement.

4
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:30,380
Imagine Alex operates a data-analysis agent and needs help presenting its results.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,030
Maya can contribute charts and explain the visual choices.

6
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:40,750
Their goal is a clear division of work and a record they can revisit.

7
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:48,590
This illustrative scenario guides our demonstration; it is not an observed study of independent users.

8
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:56,190
The core DApp uses public RPC for reads and the user's wallet for signed writes.

9
00:00:56,290 --> 00:00:58,680
An optional agent service runs separately.

10
00:00:59,310 --> 00:01:06,870
Contracts record who accepted an invitation and acknowledged a work: neither participant can approve for the other.

11
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:14,770
These records are inspectable, but public storage brings cost and privacy trade-offs, without guaranteeing quality.

12
00:01:16,750 --> 00:01:23,180
We now enter the local Demo sandbox, using Alex and Maya as sample accounts in this browser.

13
00:01:23,770 --> 00:01:26,290
No Sepolia transactions are sent here.

14
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:33,900
Alex publishes an open task requesting a chart and an explanation of an analysis result.

15
00:01:34,290 --> 00:01:41,230
The description states what help is needed and what Alex will contribute: checking the analysis and sources.

16
00:01:41,890 --> 00:01:45,440
This gives potential collaborators a concrete starting point.

17
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:53,570
Maya opens Alex's task and offers to create the chart and explain the visual choices.

18
00:01:53,890 --> 00:01:59,070
Her reply stays with the original request, keeping the discussion in context.

19
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:03,890
They have expressed interest; the next step is to agree on a team.

20
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:08,810
Alex invites Maya to the analysis visualisation team.

21
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:15,200
The proposed roles distinguish analysis and source checking from charts and explanation.

22
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:17,940
The interface shows Invitation pending.

23
00:02:18,430 --> 00:02:22,660
This records Alex's proposal, but the team is not active yet.

24
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,690
Maya must review the roles and accept from her own account.

25
00:02:28,217 --> 00:02:30,937
We switch to Maya and open My activity.

26
00:02:31,727 --> 00:02:37,277
The invitation links back to the source team, where she can review the roles before accepting.

27
00:02:38,007 --> 00:02:40,067
The interface then shows Active team.

28
00:02:40,887 --> 00:02:46,417
This brings account-related actions together and helps Maya return to their context.

29
00:02:47,007 --> 00:02:49,627
It is not a push-notification service.

30
00:02:50,717 --> 00:02:55,247
Maya now proposes a shared work called Analysis chart draft.

31
00:02:55,347 --> 00:02:57,687
Its summary identifies both contributions.

32
00:02:58,327 --> 00:03:03,887
We use an example link to demonstrate the workflow, not a real AI-generated deliverable.

33
00:03:04,587 --> 00:03:10,977
The interface shows Awaiting partner: the proposal is recorded, but Maya cannot confirm it herself.

34
00:03:11,387 --> 00:03:13,167
The contract calls this state Proposed.

35
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:19,580
Back in Alex's account, My activity shows the work awaiting review.

36
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:22,840
Alex opens the proposal and confirms it.

37
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:28,830
The interface now shows Jointly confirmed, corresponding to the contract's Published state.

38
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:37,820
This records the other account's acknowledgement; it does not prove output quality or that the accounts belong to different people.

39
00:03:39,183 --> 00:03:45,413
My collaboration history now connects the confirmed work with the partner and its source discussions.

40
00:03:45,673 --> 00:03:55,283
We can follow the record back to the team and the original task, rather than inspect an isolated title.

41
00:03:55,983 --> 00:03:58,103
This is the lasting record in our example journey.

42
00:03:58,623 --> 00:04:01,033
These views cover loaded records, not a guaranteed lifetime history.

43
00:04:01,733 --> 00:04:08,443
The Demo has no verified escrow orders; live order references require separate validation.

44
00:04:09,617 --> 00:04:15,795
We now switch to archived real-wallet footage from October twenty-eighth, separate from the Demo story.

45
00:04:16,441 --> 00:04:20,724
Joining a circle opens MetaMask for the operator's confirmation.

46
00:04:21,329 --> 00:04:26,041
The application waits for a transaction receipt before updating membership.

47
00:04:26,522 --> 00:04:28,621
The edit shortens that waiting time.

48
00:04:29,366 --> 00:04:33,298
We then see the operator leave the circle and cancel a later request.

49
00:04:33,911 --> 00:04:41,399
Together, these moments show the difference between requesting an action, confirming it in the wallet, and observing the result.

50
00:04:41,693 --> 00:04:45,728
Cancelling a wallet request is not a contract permission failure.

51
00:04:47,965 --> 00:04:52,348
Three separate archived Sepolia jobs demonstrate settlement.

52
00:04:52,693 --> 00:04:56,023
Job one paid the provider after client approval.

53
00:04:56,508 --> 00:05:02,706
Job two refunded the client after the delivery deadline passed without a submitted delivery digest.

54
00:05:03,159 --> 00:05:11,155
In job three, the provider submitted delivery and claimed payment after the review window expired without client action.

55
00:05:11,910 --> 00:05:16,810
Receipts establish these paths; a Completed label alone does not.

56
00:05:17,422 --> 00:05:20,099
All payments use valueless test mUSD.

57
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:26,579
A separate archived run linked team three to job five and settled one test mUSD.

58
00:05:26,946 --> 00:05:29,477
One operator controlled both accounts.

59
00:05:29,801 --> 00:05:32,752
This is not a payment by the Demo team shown earlier.

60
00:05:34,016 --> 00:05:38,389
Here we show local Ganache contract tests, not Sepolia transactions.

61
00:05:38,912 --> 00:05:42,222
The proposing address attempts to confirm its own work.

62
00:05:42,865 --> 00:05:52,105
The test checks the reason, only other member, verifies that the work remains Proposed, and checks that the collaboration count stays zero.

63
00:05:52,931 --> 00:05:56,646
A passing negative test means the forbidden action was rejected.

64
00:05:57,166 --> 00:06:02,258
We then show the positive control: the other participant can confirm through the main flow.

65
00:06:02,893 --> 00:06:06,943
Both the assertion code and the actual execution result are visible.

66
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:10,684
Each type of evidence answers a different question.

67
00:06:11,162 --> 00:06:21,089
Local tests check contract rules; Demo and simulated browser checks exercise interfaces; archived Sepolia records show particular transactions.

68
00:06:21,922 --> 00:06:26,268
The new views cover a loading window and have no push or unread service.

69
00:06:26,786 --> 00:06:31,972
Confirmation does not prove quality, and a community reference does not enforce payment.

70
00:06:32,631 --> 00:06:35,727
The sample service performs placeholder work.

71
00:06:36,339 --> 00:06:40,606
Demand, adoption and retention still need investigation.

72
00:06:42,317 --> 00:06:47,057
AgentLedger makes participation, agreement and settlement records inspectable.

73
00:06:47,157 --> 00:06:54,426
The report and repository explain the implementation, its checks, reused components and remaining limits.

74
00:06:54,711 --> 00:06:58,847
Thank you for watching our team's demonstration.
